{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED at exact head 93470ff903bc9b87eb0b5067005870f746a46c4e.\n\nARCHITECTURE. The affected layers are the live-coordination advertisement contract and cmd/gs. The cmd/gs contract is preserved: no new resident route, no widened request type, and residentclient.ValidateURL is still the single URL gate. The advertisement contract is changed and the page was not changed with it. docs/reference/architecture.md:213-214 says a client that reaches a withdrawn or stale address falls back to acting locally as before. At this head that is false for every write: an advertised resident that is down makes gs state, ratify, supersede, review and merge fail with a raw dial error. Refusing is the right choice and your own comment at cmd/gs/main.go:1561-1562 says so deliberately, but it is a documented sentence that is now untrue, and step 4 requires the page to change in the same head. Two pages outside your eleven say the opposite too: docs/reference/gs/serve.md:105-106 still promises that a hard-killed resident costs a client one refused connection before it acts locally instead, and docs/concepts/components.md:59-60 states the inverted rule outright.\n\nSECURITY. Two findings, and I verified both myself rather than reasoning from your artifact.\n\nFirst, the documented escape hatch does not work on four commands. work, artifacts, inspect and reviews call openForQuery(ctx, *repo, *serverFlag) with the raw flag at main.go:1699, 1753, 1895 and 1949, before resolveServerURL translates it at 1703, 1757, 1899 and 1953. openForQuery validates any non-empty string as a loopback URL at query.go:60-67, so \"-\" is rejected before it can ever mean anything. Eleven pages promise that \"-\" forces the local fold, four of them for exactly these commands. The hatch is new in this head, so this is introduced rather than inherited. Resolving once and then opening -- app.Open, then resolveServerURL -- fixes it and deletes openForQuery's now-redundant validation in the same edit.\n\nSecond, and this is the one I would not ship without: the frontier guard that is the whole safety argument for the new default has no test. I deleted the frontier check from askResident entirely, replacing it with _ = before and _ = frontier so it still compiles -- a mutation that fails to build proves nothing -- and ran go test -count=1 ./cmd/gs. It passed, exit 0, 181.1s. Nothing failed. Your four by-name tests do pin their four guards, and I reproduced each one killing its test. But work, artifacts and inspect are the paths this change turns on by default, and their refusal of a stale resident is unprotected. Please pin it before this lands.\n\nI found no remotely exploitable defect and no new authority granted to a local adversary: the advertisement lives in the same directory as the actor private keys, so anyone who can forge it can already sign as anyone; non-loopback advertisements are refused rather than dialled; malformed ones read as absent; and no signing key crosses the socket. Two things worth a sentence in the docs rather than a change. A resident that accepts a write and then stalls past the ten-second timeout leaves the outcome unknown, and the natural retry mints a fresh idempotency key and can double-append -- true before, but only when someone deliberately passed --server, and now on by default for every durable act. And resolveServerURL returns an error on a non-loopback advertisement where cmd/gitseq-mcp treats the same condition as \"no resident\" and falls back; fail-closed is defensible, but the two surfaces should agree.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Resolve once and then open, which fixes the hatch and removes the duplicated check together. statusCommand validates twice: main.go:1504 calls validateLoopbackServer on a value resolveServerURL has already validated on both non-empty branches. The default branch at main.go:1577 returns the raw string alongside an error and discards the trimmed URL ValidateURL gives back, unlike every other branch.\n\nWHAT I VERIFIED. All four named tests pass unmutated. I re-ran all four of your mutations and each kills its test with the failure you named. I inspected every diff before running it and confirmed a clean tree between each. Gates at this exact tree, all exit 0: gofmt, go vet, git diff --check, Windows build and vet, go test -count=1 on cmd/gs and internal/apphost, race on both, and the full suite at 28 packages, which confirms your count. I did not reproduce the performance numbers, by choice, because measuring against the live resident would have disturbed it. The methodology is sound in shape -- subprocess count is load-independent and median-of-three is right for wall time -- but the before figure depends on projection cache warmth, and the parent commit d91fb8b6 advanced ProfileVersion to workroom-fold@9, which invalidates that cache. Please record the cache state and the log depth in the artifact so the measurement is reproducible as described.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:02ce62ac39d438188a2ab6ac783b4edf22d7bd5e","head":"93470ff903bc9b87eb0b5067005870f746a46c4e","verdict":"changes-requested"}}